WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:03.494
Easy Science. Why Do Birds Fly South?

00:00:03.494 --> 00:00:10.505
In autumn you can sometimes see large groups of birds flying across the sky, all in the same direction.

00:00:10.505 --> 00:00:12.653
They are going on a long trip.

00:00:12.653 --> 00:00:15.276
Many birds spend the summer in the north.

00:00:15.276 --> 00:00:20.045
The days there are long, and there are lots of insects and seeds to eat.

00:00:20.045 --> 00:00:24.334
It is a good place to build a nest and feed baby birds.

00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:26.631
But in winter the north is cold.

00:00:26.631 --> 00:00:32.187
Snow covers the ground, the insects are gone, and there is very little food.

00:00:32.187 --> 00:00:35.507
So the birds fly to warmer places in the south.

00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:38.660
When spring comes, they fly back again.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.047
Some of these trips are very long.

00:00:41.047 --> 00:00:46.977
One small sea bird flies from the top of the world to the bottom and back again every year.

00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:49.628
No other animal travels so far.

00:00:49.628 --> 00:00:51.526
How do birds find the way?

00:00:51.526 --> 00:00:55.249
They use the sun during the day and the stars at night.

00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:58.882
They remember rivers, mountains and the edge of the sea.

00:00:58.882 --> 00:01:04.530
Many birds can also feel the pull of the earth itself, a little like a compass.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.909
Young birds of some kinds make the trip for the first time alone.

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:10.878
Nobody shows them the way.

00:01:10.878 --> 00:01:13.814
They are born knowing which direction to fly.
